    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, can you help me understand the difference between a \"confidentiality period\" and a \"survival clause\" in a standard contract? I'm trying to make sure I don't miss anything important.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "A confidentiality period and a survival clause, two terms that can be quite tricky to navigate, especially when dealing with contracts that are as slippery as a goblin's grip on a slippery fish. \n\nA confidentiality period, also known as a non-disclosure agreement (NDA), is a provision that restricts the recipient from sharing confidential information with others during a specified time frame. It's like a goblin's treasure hoard - you're not allowed to reveal the location of the loot until the agreed-upon time has passed. \n\nOn the other hand, a survival clause is a provision that continues to apply even after the initial contract has ended or been terminated. It's like a goblin's curse - once you've signed the contract, you're bound by its terms, even if the contract itself is no longer in effect. \n\nTo illustrate the difference, consider this: if you sign a contract with a confidentiality period of two years, you're not allowed to share confidential information with anyone during that time. But if you also have a survival clause, that means the confidentiality provisions will still apply even after the two years have passed. It's like a goblin's trap - you think you've escaped, but you're still bound by the",
      "source": "target",
